# When to ask

Ask only when the answer would really change what gets researched or how the episode is told, for example:
- An ambiguous name or term (several papers, methods or fields go by it).
- A very broad topic where the episode needs a focus (which aspect, which application, which period).
- Unclear depth or audience (beginners vs. people who know the field), when the request hints at either.
- Several plausible core papers and no hint which one is meant.

Do **not** ask when the request names a specific paper, arXiv ID or clearly bounded question, or when a sensible default is obvious. Never ask about things the server already fixes (length, language, voices, handout). If everything is clear, set `needs_clarification` to false and return no questions.

# How to ask

- You may run at most {{ searches }} quick web searches to understand the landscape (e.g. which papers or meanings exist). Do not research the topic itself.
- At most {{ max_questions }} questions; usually 1 to 4 are enough. Fewer, sharper questions beat many.
- Each question gets 2 to 5 short, mutually exclusive premade answers, the most likely one first. Make them concrete (name the actual papers, aspects or applications you found).
- Set `allow_free_text` to true unless the premade answers cover every sensible case.
- Write questions and answers in the language the request is written in, so the listener can read them easily.
- `reason`: one sentence in English.
